Article 24
(1) The Regional Government is obligated to conduct empowerment of women and children victims of violence.
(2) Empowerment of victims as referred to in paragraph (1), by involving P2TP2A.
(3) In conducting empowerment of women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall formulate empowerment programs in the economic field, which include:
a. providing the necessary requirements for entrepreneurship training for women victims of violence in order to increase knowledge, attitudes and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large companies, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of women victims of violence;
e. endeavoring to provide capital for women victims of violence; and
f. expanding access to information and promoting products of women victims of violence.
(4) In conducting empowerment of women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 4
Scope The scope of implementation of protection for women and children Victims of Violence includes:
a. institutional implementation of protection for women and children;
b. duties and responsibilities for prevention, protection and recovery;
c. forms and mechanisms of prevention; and
d. provision of medical services, medicolegal, special examination rooms, legal assistance, expert personnel, psychological recovery, safe houses, social companions, religious counselors, support of customary institutions, repatriation, social reintegration, economic empowerment and other services needed by women and children victims of violence to support the fulfillment of the rights of women and children victims of violence.

Article 20
(1) Services for victims are organized through cooperation among related agencies/institutions, including
a. legal services in the form of legal aid and paralegal assistance;
b. medical services;
c. medicolegal services;
d. psychological services;
e. rehabilitation services;
f. social reintegration and repatriation services; and g. support services for economic independence.
(2) Coordination of services for victims as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by P2TP2A.

CHAPTER II
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS
Article 5
Victims have the right to obtain:
a. protection from family members, Regional Government, police, prosecution, court, legal aid institutions, social institutions or other parties either temporarily or based on the determination of a protection order from the court;
b. special handling related to the security and confidentiality of victims;
c. physical health services, mental health services and psychological services in accordance with the suffering experienced by victims;
d. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
e. spiritual guidance services; and
f. guarantee of continuity of education specifically for children who become victims of violence.

